WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Hey Thoughty2 here,
 
Humans have always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:09.840
been enchanted by gold. We don it, devour it,&nbsp;
drink it, serenade it, even defecate atop it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:16.080
and, when the chips are down, we kill for it.&nbsp;
From King Midas to the California gold rush,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:22.620
when it comes to this most precious of precious&nbsp;
metals, we simply lose our tiny little minds.
 &nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:28.500
Given this centuries old love of all-things Au,&nbsp;
it’s no wonder that gold has remanied a target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.080
of the criminally inclined. And there was no heist&nbsp;
bigger than that of the Brink’s-Mat bullion raid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.540
in which an armed gang burst into a&nbsp;
warehouse near Heathrow airport and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:46.560
made off with £26 million worth of pure gold.
 
The heist, hailed as ‘The Crime of the Century’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.260
was at the time the largest in British&nbsp;
history. But the theft itself was merely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.940
the start of the story. Because after&nbsp;
stealing three-tonnes of solid gold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:03.060
these newly minted millionaires had to figure out&nbsp;
just what the hell they were going to do with it.
 &nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:26.820
The Brink’s-Mat raid took place at 6.30am on the&nbsp;
26th of November 1983; say what you like about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:32.820
armed robbers, but they do get to work early. 
 
The gang was led by two career criminals, Brian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:39.060
‘The Colonel’ Robinson and ‘Mad’ Mickey McAvoy,&nbsp;
one of whom was said to be the brains of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.400
operation and the other a certified hard-ass; I’ll&nbsp;
leave it to you to work out which was which. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.320
There was also an inside man, security&nbsp;
guard Anthony Black. He was married to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:54.060
Brian Robinson’ sister and he happily&nbsp;
spilled his guts to his criminal inlaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.920
about the large sums of money that were held&nbsp;
in the Brink’s-Mat warehouse where he worked.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:05.400
As far as heists go, this one was absurdly simple.&nbsp;
During his rounds, Black casually unlocked a side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:11.760
door, granting access to the seemingly secure&nbsp;
warehouse, for the rest of the crew. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.100
six balaclava-clad bandits burst in,&nbsp;
quickly subduing the security guards who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:23.340
far from ready to defend the incursion, were&nbsp;
enjoying a nice hot cup of tea and a gossip.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:29.640
Next on the to-do list was gaining access to the&nbsp;
vault. Only select guards knew the codes and Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:36.300
wasn’t one of them, so the gang politely asked one&nbsp;
of the ones who did if he wouldn’t mind handing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:42.720
over. When that didn’t work, they tried the more&nbsp;
direct route of putting a knife to his throat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:49.380
pouring petrol over his privates, and suggestively&nbsp;
lighting a match. But before the poor man had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.280
chance to cough up the codes, one of&nbsp;
the crew spotted something strange..

00:03:53.880 --> 00:04:01.380
Sitting in the middle of the warhouse were&nbsp;
several large boxes. Each concealing 6,800&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:07.500
solid gold ingots. The Brink’s-Mat robbers&nbsp;
had hoped to steal around £1 million worth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:14.460
foreign currency from the vault. Instead, they&nbsp;
found 26-times that sum just sitting there.
 &nbsp;

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:19.260
It was the heist equivalent of winning the&nbsp;
lottery, except when people win the lottery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:25.320
they aren’t usually holding a shotgun and&nbsp;
threatening to burn someone’s cock off. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.040
when you win the lottery they give you one&nbsp;
of those big novelty cheques, which must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:35.400
be a nightmare to pay into the bank, but a damn&nbsp;
sight easier than transporting 3 tonnes of gold. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:42.000
The crew had anticiapted a swift smash-and-grab&nbsp;
operation - what they hadn’t planned on is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.460
almost two hours later they’d still be loading&nbsp;
the loot into the van with a forklift truck. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:54.180
So laden with loot was the getaway vehicle&nbsp;
that they struggled to ‘get away’ at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.700
leaving the scene at a pace surpassed by&nbsp;
my gran on a midnight trip to the toilet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.040
They did manage to escape before the alarm was&nbsp;
raised, but soon enough the chase was afoot.
 &nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.960
The London police department dedicated to&nbsp;
organised crime is known as the Flying Squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:15.120
better known by its cockney rhyming&nbsp;
slang The Sweeney (Sweeney Todd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.440
Flying Squad). They had a reputation for&nbsp;
being every bit as tough as the criminals they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.240
pursued so it wasn’t long before they started&nbsp;
putting these gold bar bandits behind bars.
 &nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.020
Anthony Black, the inside man, was the first to be&nbsp;
brought in - as soon as the cops realized he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:37.380
the brother-in-law of a known criminal. He really&nbsp;
didn’t think that one through did he. Black folded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:41.700
under questioning and confessed to his part&nbsp;
in the crime. He also coughed up the names of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:47.400
ringleaders Brian Robinson and Mickey McAvoy, who&nbsp;
were both arrested only ten days after the raid. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.360
Not that either had exactly been lying&nbsp;
low in the interim - both immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:55.320
swapped their humble family homes for&nbsp;
significantly swankier properties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:00.600
purchased in cash. Just in case doubt&nbsp;
remained, McAvoy further confirmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:05.880
his culpability by trying to punch Anthony&nbsp;
Black in the face during a police line-up.
 &nbsp;

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.620
A year later Robinson and McAvoy were&nbsp;
sentenced to twenty-five years each,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:17.520
with Black getting six years. Unlike Black,&nbsp;
McAvoy and Robinson remained silent - they never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:24.120
gave up their accomplices, and the other 4 men&nbsp;
involved still haven’t been caught to this day.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:28.740
With the ringleaders safely in prison you might&nbsp;
think it would have been easy to track down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:35.460
stolen loot - after all, how the hell do you even&nbsp;
begin to launder 3 tonnes of very shiny metal?
 &nbsp;

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:41.280
Step forward John Palmer, the man the&nbsp;
press would later christen the real life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:46.620
Goldfinger - and with very good reason.
 
Despite growing up in extreme poverty in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:51.000
Birmingham, Palmer had a knack for making&nbsp;
money. He left school at a young age and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.920
started selling scrap metal and second-hand&nbsp;
cars before moving into jewellery and gold.
 &nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:01.800
By the time of the Brink’s-Mat robbery in 1983&nbsp;
Palmer was living in a Georgian mansion near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:08.220
the historic town of Bath, where he had his own&nbsp;
smelter, - a mini furnace for melting metal.
 &nbsp;

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:14.400
As a well-known criminal who owned a gold melting&nbsp;
machine,, he was the perfect person to liquidate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.540
the gang’s assets - literally. 
 
Just two days after the robbery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:23.880
Palmer’s affluent neighbours alerted the&nbsp;
police about his frantic backyard smelting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:29.400
Unsurprisingly, they figured it might have&nbsp;
something to do with the three tonnes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.540
stolen gold making news headlines. The&nbsp;
police turned up shortly afterwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:39.240
but for reasons that have been lost to history,&nbsp;
they decided not to take things any further.
 &nbsp;

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.700
That left Palmer free to melt down huge&nbsp;
quantities of gold, which he mixed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:51.000
copper to disguise its origins. He then sold it&nbsp;
on to unsuspecting companies including one called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:57.120
Johnson Matthey - the very same company&nbsp;
that owned the gold in the first place.
 &nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.500
I mean stealing someone’s gold is one&nbsp;
thing, but selling it back to them at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:06.840
an inflated price after you’ve decreased&nbsp;
the quality - that’s just criminal.
 &nbsp;

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:12.060
In total £13 million worth of profit from the gold&nbsp;
went through the accounts of Palmer’s personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:18.000
company - which he wasn’t shy about spending.
 
However ineffective the police might have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:22.920
previously, they eventually began to suspect&nbsp;
Palmer's involvement and raided his mansion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:29.940
in 1985. The trouble was, by that point it was&nbsp;
too late - he’d taken his family to Tenerife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:35.940
a “short” holiday that ended up&nbsp;
lasting two years - funny that.&nbsp;

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:42.480
He did eventually return to Blighty in 1987,&nbsp;
and was promptly put on trial for his part in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:47.700
disposing of the gold. But it seems he’d spent&nbsp;
the intervening years polishing up his case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:53.580
because, despite admitting to melting and&nbsp;
selling the gold, he was cleared of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.580 --> 00:09:00.540
charges. His defence was simple - he claimed&nbsp;
he never knew the gold was stolen. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:05.820
might sound hard to believe, but apparently the&nbsp;
jury saw things differently - Palmer left the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:11.340
courtroom that day blowing them kisses before&nbsp;
hopping on a plane straight back to Tenerife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:16.860
where he’d set up a dodgy timeshare business.
 
By this time Palmer wasn’t the only one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:21.720
the extended criminal family connected to the&nbsp;
Brink’s-Mat robbery that the police were pursuing;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:26.580
they were also after life-long gangster&nbsp;
Kenneth Noye, an expert fence who had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:31.980
reputation for being able to shift all&nbsp;
kinds of ill-gotten gains. Like Palmer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:36.480
Noye was thought to have played a pivotal part in&nbsp;
melting down the Brink’s-Mat gold and laundering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:40.560
the money which its sales brought in.
 
As a prime suspect Noye was placed under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:45.360
close police surveillance, which is how one&nbsp;
night an undercover officer came to be hiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:51.600
camouflaged in his back garden. Apparently&nbsp;
Noye was a proud horticulturalist - because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.940
on finding the police officer camped out in&nbsp;
his chrysanthemums, he stabbed him to death.At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:04.140
the subsequent trial, Noye pulled off an even&nbsp;
more unlikely victory than John Palmer, claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:11.460
he’d stabbed the police officer 10 times in the&nbsp;
chest… in self defence. He was found not guilty. 
 &nbsp;

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:15.780
Thankfully, Noye faced court again less&nbsp;
than a year later when police found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:23.460
eleven solid gold bars in his house. Even the&nbsp;
apparently easily-led jurors of 1980s England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:30.120
couldn’t ignore this glaring evidence. 
 
Noye was fined £500,000, and given fifteen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:36.480
years in prison. He was released in 1994,&nbsp;
but two years later murdered someone else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:42.720
It seems old habits quite literally died hard.
 
This time he killed a motorist in a road rage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:47.040
incident, then fled the country the following&nbsp;
day and went on the run for four years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:52.440
You may be wondering how a convicted criminal&nbsp;
wanted for murder managed to slip away so easily.&nbsp;

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:57.360
Well I assume he donned one of those pairs of&nbsp;
glasses with a false nose and moustache attached,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:03.420
but - like Ringo Starr from the Beatles -&nbsp;
Noye had a little help from his friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:09.780
only with less drumming and more stabbing.
 
In this case Noye’s friend was none other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:14.700
than old Goldfinger himself, John Palmer.&nbsp;
Perhaps there is honour among thieves after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:19.560
all. Palmer had Noye flown out of the UK&nbsp;
in his private helicopter, which took him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.660
to Palmer’s private golf course in France. From&nbsp;
there, Noye took Palmer’s Learjet to Tenerife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:30.720
where authorities lost track of him altogether.
 
Noye is believed to have orchastrated at least 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:36.000
mob murders while on the run - either&nbsp;
to evade law enforcement or to avenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:39.600
those he believed had wronged him.
 
He was eventually arrested in Spain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:43.320
and extradited back to the UK, where&nbsp;
he was sentenced to life in prison.
 &nbsp;

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:49.560
But hang on a second, did I say that John Palmer&nbsp;
owned a helicopter? And a golf course? And a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:55.080
Learjet? Yes, yes, I did. Because while&nbsp;
Kenny Noye had spent years locked up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.080 --> 00:12:00.900
Palmer had spent his time cleaning up, making an&nbsp;
absolute fortune in a scam of huge proportions.
 &nbsp;

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:06.900
You see, his holiday apartment timeshare business&nbsp;
was nothing but a huge con. It grew to be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:13.260
empire employing hundreds of people and made&nbsp;
Palmer a personal fortune of over £300 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:17.820
meaning that at one point he was as&nbsp;
rich as the actual Queen of England.
 &nbsp;

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:25.200
Palmer’s vast wealth was all built on lies&nbsp;
and up to 20,000 customers were tricked into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:30.180
paying for apartments that didn’t exist.&nbsp;
If they refused to pay, Palmer’s hired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:35.280
thugs convinced them to change their minds.
 
Not only that but Palmer was caught on camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:40.800
by legendary British investigative journalist&nbsp;
Roger Cook offering to launder £60 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:44.880
worth of drug money and boasting about how&nbsp;
he could get guns from the Russian Mafia.
 &nbsp;

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:51.480
Justice, though long overdue, eventually caught&nbsp;
up with Palmer who, just like Noye, was extradited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:56.520
back to the UK. He was put on trial, found&nbsp;
guilty, and sentenced to eight years for fraud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:03.120
He would be released after four to continue his&nbsp;
life of crime until 2015, when he was shot dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.980
outside his mansion in the English countryside.&nbsp;
No one has been convicted of his murder. 
 &nbsp;

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:13.980
The brutal end to Palmer’s life is typical of&nbsp;
many of those associated with the Brink’s-Mat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:19.380
heist, which superstitious types have&nbsp;
suggested may have been cursed. In total,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:22.980
twenty people involved in the heist&nbsp;
have been murdered over the years.&nbsp;

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:29.520
Many others have languished in prison; original&nbsp;
robbers Robison and McAvoy got long sentences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:35.160
and both died penniless, their shares of the stash&nbsp;
having evaporated by the time they were released.
 &nbsp;

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:40.380
As for the gold itself - the vast majority was&nbsp;
never recovered, and its whereabouts remain the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:46.380
subject of fevered speculation even today. So much&nbsp;
stolen gold was processed by the thieves that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:51.660
according to some estimates, almost everyone&nbsp;
who bought gold jewellery in the UK after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:59.040
1984 is wearing at least some Brink’s-Mat gold.&nbsp;
Other tales claim the laundered money has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:03.540
invested in everything from international&nbsp;
drug trafficking to the large-scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:09.720
re-development of the Docklands area of London.
 
With so many having taken their secrets to early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:15.360
graves it seems likely we will never know the&nbsp;
whole truth, and the mystery of the Brink’s-Mat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:21.240
robbery, like the gold that was taken that&nbsp;
day, will continue to fascinate and tantalise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:24.480
us for years to come.
 
Thanks for watching.

